content.text:
Potential issue: `this.prev[slot]` adds pressed for held touch; fine. But after touchUp, held disappears. Good.

Now main.js. Need add properties, elements, init reading, bind events, setup methods, sync calls.

Let's inspect main.js around constructor and showScreen/setPaused/startBattle. We'll patch carefully.

Add properties after `cards`:
```
this.touchEnabled = false;
this.touchPointers = new Map();
this.touchCounts = new Map();
this.touchHintShown = false;
this.systemButtons = $('.system-buttons');
```
Maybe compute default in init after localStorage.

Elements add:
```
touchControls: $('#touchControls'),
touchToggleBtn: $('#touchToggleBtn'),
battlePauseBtn: $('#battlePauseBtn'),
```
Need `#battlePauseBtn` exists.

Init:
- After input.attach, determine touch support and preference. Do before syncSound:
```
this.initTouchPrefs();
```
But `setupTouchControls` needs DOM; all exists.
```
const coarse = window.matchMedia?.('(pointer: coarse)').matches || navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0;
let pref = null; try { pref = localStorage.getItem('smash-arena-touch'); } catch {}
this.touchEnabled = pref ? pref === 'on' : coarse;
```
Add method `initTouch()` that does the above plus bindings. Could call `this.setupTouchControls()` in init after bindEvents? Order doesn't matter. Let's implement `setupTouchControls` and `syncTouch`.
- Need media query if device changes? Not necessary. Maybe listen for pointer changes? no.
- For Playwright mobile with hasTouch, `matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)')` should be true. Good.

Bind events:
- `#battlePauseBtn` click: `if (this.screen === 'battle') this.setPaused(!this.paused);` But when paused, the button is hidden by modal system buttons; no issue.
- `#touchToggleBtn` click: `this.setTouchEnabled(!this.touchEnabled);`
- The touch action buttons and pause overlay handled in setup.

ShowScreen: after setting classes, call `this.syncTouch()` at end? Must ensure `this.screen` set first. There are branches setting music etc. Add at end of method. But on title, syncTouch should hide overlay; yes.
- `setPaused`: after toggling, call syncTouch.
- `startBattle`: set input.touchSlot to first human slot, maybe show toast "TOUCH CONTROLS ON" if enabled and coarse? Actually after start showScreen battle -> sync shows. Could add `if (this.touchEnabled) this.toast('TOUCH CONTROLS ON · PAUSE TO HIDE', 2400)` but startBattle already shows a toast with controls hint. The toast would override; not needed.
- `showResults`: screen results, showScreen calls sync. Good.
- `setTouchEnabled`: set, save, sync, toast. If disabling while overlay visible, release touch. If enabling while paused, overlay hidden, toast. If enabling on desktop, show.
- `syncTouch`:
```
syncTouch() {
  const on = Boolean(this.touchEnabled && this.screen === 'battle' && !this.paused);
  const overlay = this.els.touchControls;
  if (overlay) {
    overlay.classList.toggle('hidden', !on);
    overlay.setAttribute('aria-hidden', on ? 'false' : 'true');
  }
  const app = document.querySelector('#app');
  if (app) app.classList.toggle('touch-mode', Boolean(this.touchEnabled));
  const toggle = this.els.touchToggleBtn;
  if (toggle) {
    toggle.innerHTML = `Touch Controls <b>${this.touchEnabled ? 'ON' : 'OFF'}</b>`;
    toggle.classList.toggle('on', this.touchEnabled); // optional CSS
  }
  if (this.els.battlePauseBtn) this.els.battlePauseBtn.classList.toggle('hidden', this.screen !== 'battle');
  if (!on) this.releaseTouchButtons();
}
```
But `releaseTouchButtons` should also call `this.input.releaseTouch()`; to prevent stuck actions when paused/results. Do:
```
releaseTouchButtons() {
  this.touchPointers.clear();
  this.touchCounts.clear();
  $$('#touchControls .pressed').forEach(el => el.classList.remove('pressed'));
  this.input.releaseTouch();
}
```
Potential: `syncTouch` is called every showScreen/setPaused, but not every frame. When not on, release only once. Fine.
- On `window.blur`, input.releaseAll but the button pressed visual remains. Add window blur listener? InputManager doesn't notify. Could add in app:
```
window.addEventListener('blur', () => this.releaseTouchButtons());
```
Not critical, but good.
- `setupTouchControls`:
```
const buttons = $$('#touchControls [data-touch]');
for (const btn of buttons) {
  const action = btn.dataset.touch;
  const press = (e) => {
    if (!this.touchEnabled || this.screen !== 'battle' || this.paused) return;
    e.preventDefault();
    if (e.pointerId !== undefined) {
      this.touchPointers.set(e.pointerId, action);
      try { btn.setPointerCapture(e.pointerId); } catch {}
    }
    const count = (this.touchCounts.get(action) || 0) + 1;
    this.touchCounts.set(action, count);
    btn.classList.add('pressed');
    this.input.touchDown(action);
  };
  const release = (e) => {
    const tracked = e.pointerId !== undefined ? this.touchPointers.get(e.pointerId) : action;
    if (tracked !== action) return;
    e.preventDefault();
    this.touchPointers.delete(e.pointerId);
    const count = Math.max(0, (this.touchCounts.get(action) || 1) - 1);
    if (count === 0) {
      this.touchCounts.delete(action);
      btn.classList.remove('pressed');
      this.input.touchUp(action);
    } else {
      this.touchCounts.set(action, count);
    }
  };
  btn.addEventListener('pointerdown', press);
  btn.addEventListener('pointerup', release);
  btn.addEventListener('pointercancel', release);
  btn.addEventListener('lostpointercapture', release);
  btn.addEventListener('contextmenu', (e) => e.preventDefault());
}
```
Issue: if no pointerId (keyboard click), tracked=action; works. `lostpointercapture` may fire after pointerup; then tracked is already deleted -> tracked undefined !== action, return. Good.
- `pointerdown` on a button may also trigger a `pointerup` on window if released outside; capture helps.
- Add `pointerleave`? Not with capture. If pointer capture unavailable, pointerup outside may not fire. Could add `window.addEventListener('pointerup', ...)` to clear all by pointerId. Better robust:
```
window.addEventListener('pointerup', this._onTouchRelease, true);
window.addEventListener('pointercancel', ...);
```
But we already per-button; global listener can handle releases outside. Implement methods `endTouchPointer(e)` that uses map and count. However, per-button pointerup events don't bubble if capture? They bubble to window. We can attach window listener and avoid per-button duplication. Let's design:
   - `press` on each button: record pointerId -> action; counts; etc.
   - Global window `pointerup`/`pointercancel` listener: for each ended pointer, look up action in map, decrement count, remove pressed class.
   - But if pointerup on button, global handler catches. Use `{capture:true}`.
   - If pointerup not captured, still global. Good.
   - Remove per-button pointerup? Keep pointercancel and lostpointercapture? Lost capture may fire after pointerup; map already deleted, no-op. Simpler: per-button pointerdown; global release.
   - `window.addEventListener('pointerup', this._endTouchPointer, true)` and pointercancel. Bind method.
